  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "concept": "introduce the theorem",
      "narration": "The Pythagorean Theorem helps find the length of a side in a right triangle. It says that the square of the longest side equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides. This is useful for solving real-world problems like measuring distances.",
      "board": [
        {
          "id": "a1",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_text",
          "text": "Pythagorean Theorem",
          "region": "title",
          "rect": null,
          "style": {
            "font_size": 1.0,
            "color": "chalk",
            "bold": false,
            "underline": false,
            "align": "left"
          },
          "speed": 0.045
        },
        {
          "id": "a2",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_math",
          "latex": "c^2 = a^2 + b^2",
          "region": "auto",
          "rect": {
            "x": 0.06,
            "y": 0.22,
            "w": 0.4,
            "h": 0.14
          },
          "font_size": 1.4,
          "color": "math",
          "speed": 0.06
        }
      ],
      "checkpoint": null
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "concept": "explain the parts",
      "narration": "In a right triangle, 'c' is the hypotenuse, the longest side opposite the right angle. 'a' and 'b' are the legs, the other two sides. The theorem connects these three sides mathematically.",
      "board": [
        {
          "id": "a3",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_text",
          "text": "Right Triangle",
          "region": "title",
          "rect": null,
          "style": {
            "font_size": 1.0,
            "color": "chalk",
            "bold": false,
            "underline": false,
            "align": "left"
          },
          "speed": 0.045
        },
        {
          "id": "a5",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_text",
          "text": "c = hypotenuse",
          "region": "title",
          "rect": null,
          "style": {
            "font_size": 1.0,
            "color": "chalk",
            "bold": false,
            "underline": false,
            "align": "left"
          },
          "speed": 0.045
        },
        {
          "id": "a6",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_text",
          "text": "a and b = legs",
          "region": "title",
          "rect": null,
          "style": {
            "font_size": 1.0,
            "color": "chalk",
            "bold": false,
            "underline": false,
            "align": "left"
          },
          "speed": 0.045
        }
      ],
      "checkpoint": null
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "concept": "show a worked example",
      "narration": "Suppose a triangle has legs of 3 units and 4 units. Then the hypotenuse is found using c^2 = 3^2 + 4^2. That gives c^2 = 9 + 16 = 25, so c = 5 units.",
      "board": [
        {
          "id": "a7",
          "duration_ms": 800,
          "start": null,
          "op": "write_math",
          "latex": "c^2 = 3^2 + 4^2",
